SPEAR documentation
======================

Sphinx documentation for SPEAR. Layout, configuration and landing page
follow the shared model of this family of projects: doc/Makefile, sources in
doc/source/*.rst, configuration in doc/source/conf.py, output in doc/build/
(generated — never edit it, never index it).

Structure
---------

Top-level pages are flat; a topic with several pages gets a directory and an
eponymous index (harness/harness.rst, model/model.rst), the way the sibling
projects group their multi-page topics.

index.rst carries the REDS/HEIG-VD logotype and the landing prose. The
toctrees are hidden, numbered and captioned, one per part of the manual, and
the landing sections below them are definition lists of cross-references.
Every page therefore needs a label (`.. _name:` above its title) so those
references are `:ref:` and break the build if their target disappears.

Cross-references are absolute (:doc:`/harness/sandbox`), and so are image
paths from a subdirectory (/img/...). A relative reference changes meaning
with the directory of the page that writes it, which is exactly what moving
a page does.

Warnings are errors
-------------------

CI builds with `sphinx-build -W --keep-going`. Without -W, an extension that
cannot reach its native tool only logs a warning and drops the node: the job
stays green while the diagrams are missing from the published pages.

To check warnings reliably, clean first — an incremental build skips the
consistency check:

    make clean && make html

Build
-----

    make html                 # -> build/html/index.html
    make latexpdf             # -> build/latex/SPEAR.pdf
    make clean

Sphinx and the RTD theme come from the system Python (/usr/bin/sphinx-build),
not from the spear virtualenv, which carries only the application's
runtime dependencies.

Diagrams
--------

    make diagrams             # regenerates source/img/spear.drawio AND the SVGs

source/img/gen_spear_diagrams.py is the single description of every diagram.
Running it writes two things from that one description:

  * spear.drawio        the editable multi-page drawio document (one page per
                        diagram) — open it in draw.io or the VS Code extension
                        and every box is editable;
  * spear_<page>.svg    the rendered diagrams the HTML build consumes.

Keep structural changes in the generator: editing the .drawio by hand works,
but a subsequent `make diagrams` regenerates it.

Why the SVGs are not exported from the .drawio
----------------------------------------------

export_png.sh drives the drawio CLI and is kept for environments where that
CLI works. On this machine it does not: the drawio snap 30.4.1 fails on every
input with

    Error reading file: <path>
    ReferenceError: next is not defined
        at processOneFile (.../app.asar/src/main/electron.js:1256:8)

including a five-element minimal file and the SO3 documentation's own
diagrams. Rendering the SVGs directly from the generator keeps the
documentation build independent of that upstream bug, and SVG stays crisp at
any zoom.

The version string
------------------

conf.py derives `release` from spear/active-model.conf, so the served
model appears in the page header and never has to be bumped by hand.
